Your draft has a strong foundation, but one conceptual correction is essential:

Microsoft 365 → Fabric → Power BI is not a strict administrative hierarchy.

Microsoft 365 and Microsoft Entra provide identity, groups, licenses, roles, security policies, and tenant-level cloud administration. Fabric provides the analytics administration and governance plane. Power BI is one of the workloads administered within Fabric. Domains and capacities are two parallel ways of organizing Fabric: domains represent business ownership, while capacities represent compute resources.

1. Recommended document purpose

Replace the existing purpose with:

Purpose

The purpose of this document is to define the operating model, governance structure, administrative roles, organizational ownership, workspace standards, security controls, and lifecycle processes used to administer Microsoft Fabric and the Power BI workload within the organization’s Microsoft cloud tenant.

The document establishes:

How Microsoft 365, Microsoft Entra ID, Fabric, and Power BI administrative responsibilities interact.
How Fabric domains and subdomains represent the organizational structure.
How workspaces are requested, approved, created, secured, operated, monitored, and retired.
How business owners, domain stewards, IT representatives, Fabric administrators, and workspace administrators share responsibility.
How users obtain access to workspaces, Power BI apps, reports, semantic models, and data.
How naming, environments, deployment, monitoring, and access recertification are standardized.
