3. Revised glossary
Microsoft 365 tenant

The Microsoft 365 tenant represents the organization’s Microsoft cloud environment. It contains users, groups, administrative roles, subscriptions, licenses, registered domains, applications, and security configurations.

For the Fabric and Power BI operating model, Microsoft 365 and Microsoft Entra are responsible primarily for:

User identities.
Security and Microsoft 365 groups.
Administrative role assignment.
Fabric and Power BI license assignment.
Service principals and application identities.
Authentication and Conditional Access.
User onboarding and offboarding.

Most Fabric administrative roles are assigned through the Microsoft 365 administration environment. Dedicated Fabric administration roles allow an organization to administer Fabric without granting unrestricted Global Administrator permissions.

Microsoft Fabric

Microsoft Fabric is Microsoft’s unified analytics SaaS platform. It provides shared administration, governance, storage, computing, monitoring, and security capabilities for workloads such as Power BI, Data Engineering, Data Factory, Data Science, Data Warehouse, Real-Time Intelligence, and databases.

Fabric administration includes:

Tenant settings.
Workspace administration.
Domain administration.
Capacity administration.
Feature enablement.
Security and sharing controls.
Audit and usage monitoring.
Administrative APIs.
Governance and information protection integration.

The Fabric administration portal is the central administrative interface, but some related functions remain in Microsoft 365, Entra, Azure, and Microsoft Purview.

Power BI

Power BI is the business intelligence and reporting workload within Microsoft Fabric. It is used to create semantic models, reports, dashboards, metrics, and applications and to distribute analytical information to users.

Power BI administration is now largely performed through the Fabric administration model. A “Power BI Administration Account” should not be described as a separate account type. It is normally a Microsoft Entra organizational account assigned the Fabric Administrator or, where organizationally appropriate, Power Platform Administrator role. Both roles can perform organization-wide Fabric administration tasks, except that licensing remains under Microsoft 365 licensing administration.

Capacity

A capacity is the compute resource used to run Fabric workloads. Capacity management is separate from business-domain management.

Capacity administration includes:

Assigning workspaces to a capacity.
Managing access to the capacity.
Monitoring consumption and performance.
Configuring workload resource behavior.
Reviewing throttling and overload.
Supporting performance and cost management.

A Capacity Administrator controls only the capacity assigned to them and does not automatically become a Fabric tenant administrator.

Domain

A Fabric domain is a logical governance structure used to group workspaces that belong to a business function, organizational area, or data-ownership boundary.

Domains do not contain reports or data directly. They organize and govern the workspaces that contain those items.

A domain can contain subdomains. For this organization, the four principal organizational groups should become the top-level domains, and their areas should become subdomains. Fabric Administrators create top-level domains, while Fabric Administrators or Domain Administrators can create subdomains.

Subdomain

A subdomain represents a more specific organizational area within a domain.

For example:

Corporate Services
    ├── Finance
    ├── Human Resources
    ├── Quality
    └── Sustainability and Environment

The subdomain is the most appropriate level for associating the Responsible and Accountable people supplied in your organizational table.

Workspace

A workspace is the primary collaboration, security, and content-management container in Fabric.

It can contain:

Lakehouses.
Warehouses.
Pipelines.
Dataflows.
Notebooks.
Semantic models.
Reports.
Dashboards.
Power BI apps.
Other Fabric items.

A workspace has its own access roles, settings, capacity assignment, domain assignment, contacts, description, and lifecycle.

Item

An item is an individual Fabric or Power BI asset stored inside a workspace, such as a report, semantic model, notebook, lakehouse, warehouse, pipeline, or dashboard.

Item-level permissions may complement workspace and app access, but excessive direct item sharing should be avoided because it makes access governance more difficult.

Power BI app

A Power BI app is the preferred mechanism for distributing governed reports and dashboards to business consumers.

Apps can contain multiple audiences. Each audience can see a different subset of reports and can be assigned to Entra security groups or other supported groups. Consumers generally should receive app access rather than workspace access unless they need to collaborate on content.

