Fabric and Power BI administration — revised sections

Revision of the first draft. Replaces the Glossary, the Domain / Workspace / Role definitions, the convention section and the diagrams. Everything else in the original draft still stands.

1. Glossary and administrative model
1.1 Microsoft Entra ID

The identity layer. Every principal that touches Fabric or Power BI — user, security group, or service principal — is an Entra object.

The single most misunderstood point in this platform: the Fabric administrative roles are Entra roles, not Fabric roles. They are assigned in the Microsoft 365 admin center or the Entra portal. There is no "make this person an admin" button inside the Fabric portal.

1.2 Microsoft 365 tenant

The organization's cloud environment. One tenant = one Fabric environment = one Power BI environment. There is no way to run two independent Fabric estates inside one tenant; separation is achieved through capacities, domains and workspaces, not through additional tenants.

Licensing (Fabric Free, Pro, Premium Per User, F SKUs) is managed here, and licensing is the one thing a Fabric administrator cannot do.

Relevant roles:

Role	Scope	Use
Global administrator	Everything, including all of Fabric	Emergency access only. Should never be the day-to-day BI administrator.
Power Platform administrator	Superset that includes full Fabric administration	Only where Power Platform and Fabric are administered by the same team.
Fabric administrator	Fabric and Power BI tenant administration	The recommended least-privilege role for this platform.
1.3 Fabric administrator

The Entra role formerly called Power BI Service Administrator. Same role, renamed when Power BI became a workload inside Fabric. Documentation and blog posts written before the rename use the old name for the same thing.

Grants:

Access to the Fabric admin portal and all tenant settings
Capacity settings and capacity assignment
Creation and deletion of domains, and assignment of domain admins
Tenant-wide workspace visibility and management, including workspaces the admin is not a member of
Usage metrics, the admin APIs, and the activity log
The ability to delegate specific tenant settings downward

Does not grant:

Licence assignment or billing (Microsoft 365 admin / billing)
User and group creation (Entra)
Access to the content of a workspace by default — administrative visibility is not the same as data access

Operational gotcha worth writing into the runbook: an admin account still needs a Fabric licence assigned (Fabric Free is enough) before the admin portal will open. Cloud-only admin accounts created without any licence hit a "your organisation has blocked access" redirect that looks like a permissions bug and is not.

1.4 Capacity admin

Assigned per capacity, at the moment the capacity is created. Manages workload settings, scale, and which workspaces are attached to that capacity. Can override tenant settings that have been delegated to capacity level, affecting only the workspaces on that capacity. This role is not tenant-wide.

1.5 Domain and subdomain

A domain is a logical grouping of workspaces by business area. It is the delegation point of the model, and it supports one level of subdomain beneath it.

Three roles:

Role	Can	Cannot
Fabric administrator	Create, rename and delete domains; assign domain admins; assign any workspace to any domain	—
Domain admin	Update description and image, manage subdomains, define domain contributors, assign workspaces to the domain, override delegated tenant settings for the domain	Delete the domain, rename it, add or remove other domain admins
Domain contributor	A workspace admin authorised to assign their own workspace to the domain, from workspace settings	Anything else at domain level

Critical clarification for the document: domain assignment does not grant, restrict or change access to anything. Item discovery, visibility and access depend on workspace role and item permissions. What a domain changes is organisation, discoverability in the OneLake catalog, and which delegated settings apply. Anyone who reads the domain model as a security model will design the wrong thing.

Second clarification: the set of tenant settings that can actually be delegated to domain level is short and changes between releases. Confirm the current list in the tenant settings search before promising a business area that it can self-manage a specific setting.

1.6 Workspace

The container. Holds items, carries the four fixed roles, sits on one capacity, and belongs to at most one domain.

Role	Key capability	Key limitation
Admin	Everything, including access management and deletion	—
Member	Publish, share, add others up to Member	Cannot delete the workspace
Contributor	Create and edit content	Cannot share or manage access
Viewer	Read published content; RLS is enforced	Needs Build permission separately to create their own reports on a semantic model
1.7 Power BI

Power BI is a workload inside Fabric, not a separate product with its own administration. The Power BI admin portal is the Fabric admin portal. Power BI specific controls (Premium Per User, embed codes, dashboard and report settings, export and sharing switches) are a subsection of Fabric tenant settings.

There is no separate "Power BI administration account". Remove that term from the draft.

1.8 Service principal

An Entra application identity used for automation. This is the mechanism for everything recurring in this programme: workspace inventory, orphan detection, role auditing, provisioning, recertification reminders.

Two tenant settings gate it, both scoped to a security group:

Service principals can use Fabric APIs
Service principals can use read-only Fabric admin APIs

Recommendation: one service principal for read-only inventory and monitoring, a second, separately governed one for write operations (provisioning, role assignment, deletion). Do not use a named human Fabric administrator account for automation — it breaks when the person leaves, which is the same failure that produced the 4,000 orphaned workspaces.

1.9 The delegation chain

Tenant setting → delegated to a domain or a capacity → optionally delegated further to a workspace. A tenant admin can bypass the middle and delegate straight to a workspace. Overrides are scoped: a change at domain level affects only workspaces in that domain; at capacity level, only workspaces on that capacity.

